# The reviewer /ship runs for step 7, the security pass, when the gate is a
# separate one. A combined gate has no step 7: its one review covers the
# security areas too, and this value goes unused on that branch. Two shapes:
# agent:<name> dispatches that agent on this host, and anything else is a
# command line the audit prompt is appended to.
#
# `auto` means Codex with gpt-5.6-sol, read-only, in a fresh session, told not to
# read the reviewed repository's own instructions. That is the one reviewer that
# has been put to the test: it was run against tests/fixtures/security-review,
# found both planted Critical defects with the attacker, the input and the gain,
# and invented nothing against the clean counterpart. Nothing else here has been
# qualified, so nothing else is chosen for you. A host without codex stops the
# gate and names this file, rather than running an unqualified reviewer and
# calling the result a security pass.
#
# If Sol is refused with an HTTP 400, /ship retries once with gpt-5.6-terra and
# says in the pull request which reviewer actually ran. A review that silently
# downgraded is worse than one that did not happen.
#
# No agent is chosen automatically. An agent is dispatched by bare name into the
# repository being reviewed, which can hold a definition of that name, and the
# gate cannot tell which of the two it would run. You may still state
# agent:<name> here; the gate then refuses if the repository being reviewed holds
# .claude/agents/<name>.md, so remove that file from the worktree or name a
# command line instead. An explicit agent: value on a host that cannot dispatch
# agents stops the gate rather than running something else.
#
# Copy this file to config/security-reviewer to change it: /ship reads config/
# first and falls back here.
auto
